<<~ ahu slot=`#/contract`>>

!! TW5 Boot Gate — Contract

`bootTrustedModules(tw)` (`packages/lararium-tw5/src/tw5-module-gate.ts`) runs inside `TW5Engine.boot`, in the host boot callback. It adds JavaScript tiddlers from the ''live corpus'' (tiddlers the wiki holds from `preloadTiddlers`), after TW5 completes its own module collection — so execution of a gated body during that boot reads UNBUILT. When no corpus meme passes the gate it injects nothing; the gate carries no imperative fallback of its own.

```
bootTrustedModules(tw)
    │
    ├─ wiki.filterTiddlers([all[tiddlers]stack:has[MODULE_COMPONENT_URI]])
    │       │  for each matching tiddler:
    │       ├─ Layer 1: mana ≥ 18 · manao ≥ 17 · manaoio ≥ 17 (an unparseable level fails)
    │       ├─ skip an empty body
    │       ├─ Layer 2: tagged SHA-256(text) equals the source-sha256 field
    │       └─ Layer 3: keyhive capability proof — UNBUILT
    │
    ├─ each pass → wiki.addTiddler({ type: application/javascript, module-type, text })
    │
    ├─ if any passed → tw.modules.define(moduleText, "library", "lararium-tw5-modules")
    │                  (TW5 reads the first argument as the module NAME, so the aggregate
    │                   body does not become the library definition)
    │
    └─ if none passed → nothing injects; the packed plugin's own module tiddlers
                        carry the parser, the deserializer and the widgets
```

''Why two paths?'' The corpus path names the target steady state: all logic lives as `lar:` memes, verified by hash and (UNBUILT) keyhive capability proof. The packed plugin keeps the app booting and working offline, on cold start, and before any meme passes every gate layer.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/thresholds`>>

!! Gate Thresholds

Three signal fields must meet or exceed these floor values before the gate injects a meme as a live JS module. The constants live in `packages/lararium-tw5/src/tw5-module-gate.ts` as `MODULE_*_THRESHOLD`.

| Field | Constant | Floor |
|---|---|---|
| `mana` | `MODULE_MANA_THRESHOLD` | 18 |
| `manao` | `MODULE_MANAO_THRESHOLD` | 17 |
| `manaoio` | `MODULE_MANAOIO_THRESHOLD` | 17 |
| `confidence` | — | UNBUILT: no `MODULE_CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD` stands, and the gate reads no `confidence` field |

Additionally: `source-sha256` must verify (the tagged SHA-256 digest of the `text` field). Gate layer 3 (keyhive capability proof) reads UNBUILT; as built the gate passes on layers 1–2 alone.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/stuck-in-ts`>>

!! Why No Meme Can Carry This

`bootTrustedModules` requires a live `$tw` reference to call `wiki.filterTiddlers()`, `wiki.addTiddler()`, and `tw.modules.define()` — runtime calls against the TW5 instance `boot()` creates. No wikitext expresses the gate logic and no tiddler loads it: the gate decides which memes become executable.

''The gate queries a COMPONENT, and a meme carries that component'': `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/tw5/tw5-module`. The gate asks `[stack:has[…]]` — which carriers WEAR it — never what kind a carrier claims. Any carrier wearing that component, passing the signal fields and the content hash, gets injected by the corpus path.

The distinction makes the whole gate. ''Authority rides on what a carrier holds and who granted it, never on its kind'': a carrier cannot declare itself executable, it can only wear a component whose own meme states what wearing it costs, and the stack entry carries no verb of its own.

<<~/ahu>>

